                    coach_default:          `Call committed.`,
                    coach_r1_acquire:       `<strong>Acquisition.</strong> You own the IP and the talent now, and you ship before the cliff — the €16M of ARR is safe. But you've never integrated a foreign, research-led culture, and the value you're buying walks out of the building every night. What does ownership buy you that an alliance doesn't — and is it worth €70M more and the integration risk? The next two rounds decide whether this was bold or value-destroying.`,
                    coach_r1_alliance:      `<strong>Alliance.</strong> You get most of the capability for a quarter of the cash, you ship before the cliff, and it's reversible — strong strategic agility. The cost: you neither own the IP nor control the roadmap, and the partner could later be bought by the rival. Watch that exposure in Round 4.`,
                    coach_r1_build:         `<strong>Organic build.</strong> Full control, no culture clash — and a 20–24-month time-to-ship. Your three at-risk customers, €16M of ARR, renew <em>before</em> that. You'll have a beautiful module and no one to sell it to, because the rival took them. Speed to the cliff was the whole point. Does build survive that constraint?`,
                    coach_r1_build_contradiction: `<strong>You named speed as the deciding factor — then chose to build.</strong> Build ships in 20–24 months; the cliff is ~12. If speed truly decides it, build is the one mode that fails the test. Mode choice is dominated by time-to-value when the cliff is real.`,
                    coach_r1_boldness:      `<strong>You're acquiring because it's the boldest, most decisive move.</strong> Maybe. But an alliance gets you most of the capability for a quarter of the cash and is reversible. The boldest mode is not automatically the value-maximizing one — decisiveness is not a deal thesis.`,
                    coach_r1_cost:          `<strong>“Lowest cash outlay” and a €95M acquisition don't belong in the same sentence.</strong> If preserving the war chest is the deciding factor, the acquisition is the one path that contradicts it. Let the real deciding variable — speed to the cliff or IP control — actually drive the mode.`,
                    coach_r2_winnerscurse:  `<strong>You sized the synergy bridge above ~€13M to justify the price.</strong> Revenue and cost synergies are routinely over-estimated and integration costs under-estimated. When the claim collapses to what you can actually deliver, you didn't buy a bargain — you bought the winner's curse against the €12M floor.`,
                    coach_r2_reprice:       `<strong>You structured an earn-out tied to retention and synergy delivery.</strong> Exactly right: the architect built 40% of the model and has no lock-in, so you've moved the key-person risk off your balance sheet and protected the value you're paying for. Disciplined valuation, not just disciplined logic.`,
                    coach_r2_adjust:        `<strong>Adjust.</strong> You refused to let a 9.5x-revenue premium rest on unsized synergy. The €95M only works if the synergy is both credible and structurally protected — and now it is. This is the disciplined call the board's floor demands.`,
                    coach_r2_ignore:        `<strong>You waved the diligence finding through.</strong> The lead architect built 40% of the model and can walk on a Friday. “The technology is what matters, not one person” is precisely the assumption that destroys tech acquisitions — the technology lives in the people.`,
                    coach_r2_payask:        `<strong>You paid €95M for €10M of revenue and no profit, with the synergy unsized.</strong> That's 9.5x revenue justified entirely by synergy you haven't proven. If realistic synergy is €8M, you didn't buy a bargain — you bought the winner's curse, and the NPV just told you so.`,
                    coach_r2_walk:          `<strong>You walked from the price and pivoted to the cheaper, reversible mode.</strong> Disciplined: if the bridge can't clear €12M at €95M, refusing the winner's curse protects capital — and the alliance still ships before the cliff. Strategic agility in action.`,
                    coach_r3_calibrated:    `<strong>A calibrated integration depth.</strong> Deep enough to combine the AI module and cross-sell it, shallow enough to keep the research culture intact. This is the point on the preservation→absorption spectrum where synergy moves <em>and</em> the engineers stay — exactly the balance Gomes's research says decides the deal.`,
                    coach_r3_ringfence:     `<strong>You ring-fenced NordCast to keep its people happy — and forfeited the synergy.</strong> There's now no mechanism to combine the AI module with your platform or cross-sell it. You preserved the culture and the board funded €12M of value, not a happy, disconnected subsidiary. Preservation has a synergy cost.`,
                    coach_r3_absorb:        `<strong>Full absorption in six months gets you to €12M fastest — on paper.</strong> It's also exactly when 30–50% of the acquired engineers leave. Capture synergy from people who've quit and your €12M is fiction. The value you bought is the talent; you just integrated it out the door.`,
                    coach_r3_noplan:        `<strong>No retention or cultural-fit plan.</strong> You bought 60 research-led engineers and gave them no reason to stay. “The technology transfers regardless” is the winner's-curse assumption in its purest form — the technology is the people, and they're flight-risk.`,
                    coach_r3_attrition:     `<strong>Attrition just crossed the danger line.</strong> You pushed depth and timeline harder than the retention plan could absorb, and the engineers are leaving. Every point of synergy you modeled is now gated by who's still in the building — and fewer of them are.`,
                    coach_r4_accelerate:    `<strong>You activated retention triggers and promoted internal successors.</strong> Because enough of the team was still there, accelerating the joint pod re-secured the capability rather than papering over a hole. Agility means you anticipated this and had a move ready.`,
                    coach_r4_accelerate_thin: `<strong>You moved to accelerate — but there's barely anyone left to accelerate.</strong> The earlier attrition hollowed the team, so the retention triggers fire into an empty room. The move was right; the talent base that makes it work was already gone.`,
                    coach_r4_renegotiate:   `<strong>You renegotiated to re-secure the capability.</strong> Restructuring terms under the shock is a credible agile response — it buys back control without the cost of a full mode switch. Just make sure the synergy math still clears the floor after the new terms.`,
                    coach_r4_pivot:         `<strong>You pivoted modes under the shock.</strong> Because you preserved reversibility in Round 1, converting the alliance (or acqui-hiring the team) was a live option rather than a panic. This is exactly the strategic agility Gomes's lens prizes: committed, but able to switch when the environment moved.`,
                    coach_r4_pivot_trapped: `<strong>You tried to pivot — but you burned the boats in Round 1.</strong> With reversibility weighted low, switching modes now is costly and slow. Agility isn't a Round-4 decision; it's an option you either preserved earlier or you didn't.`,
                    coach_r4_freeze:        `<strong>You stood still.</strong> The architect quit and the rival bid for your partner, and you held the plan. Standing still is a decision too — and it's the one that loses the capability you committed €95M to secure. Agility means having a move; you had none.`,
                    coach_r4_strategic_ok:  `<strong>You led with strategic logic — and the number happens to back it.</strong> The capability did secure the segment, and the synergy cleared the floor, so the logic isn't a smokescreen. Fine, but the board funded a number; lead with the bridge next time and the logic lands harder.`,
                    coach_r4_strategic_short: `<strong>You led with strategic logic to cover a missed floor.</strong> “It secured the segment” is what every value-destroying deal says at the board review. The board funded €12M of run-rate value, not a narrative. Strategic logic can't paper over synergy you didn't deliver.`,
                    coach_r4_defendprice:   `<strong>You defended the 9.5x-revenue price as fair.</strong> That's the winner's curse arguing for itself. Price was never the question — realized synergy against the €12M floor was. Defending the premium tells the board you still don't see what destroyed the value.`,
                    coach_r4_rattled:       `<strong>You were rattled under the board's probing.</strong> They pressed every synergy assumption and you got defensive instead of owning the numbers. A corporate-development lead who can't hold the room on their own bridge loses the benefit of the doubt on the deal.`,

                    sum_default:            `Call made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Chose a mode that ships before the cliff; decided on speed.`,
